# Passkeys (WebAuthn) backend TODO

Generated by the light-it auth package. The registration and login ceremonies, the
`passkeys` migration, and the six API routes are wired for you. The four items below
are environment-specific and cannot be verified at scaffold time — read all of them
before your first deploy. Delete this file once you've acted on it.

## 1. Secure context is required — WebAuthn will not run at all otherwise

`navigator.credentials.create()` and `navigator.credentials.get()` are only defined
in a **secure context**. Outside of one, `window.PublicKeyCredential` is `undefined`
and calling either method throws a `TypeError` — it does not degrade, warn, or fall
back, it refuses to run.

| Dev URL | Works? |
| --- | --- |
| `http://localhost:5173` (Vite default) | Yes — `localhost` is trustworthy even over plain HTTP |
| `http://127.0.0.1:5173` | Yes — same rule |
| `https://myapp.test` (mkcert / Herd / Valet `secure`) | Yes — secure context |
| `http://myapp.test` (custom TLD over plain HTTP) | **No** |
| `http://192.168.1.20:5173` (LAN IP, e.g. testing on a phone) | **No** — this is the one that bites |

**Action required, not a warning to note and move on from:** if your local dev URL is
not `localhost`/`127.0.0.1` and not already HTTPS, do one of:

- Run `mkcert -install && mkcert myapp.test` (or the equivalent step for your own
  local TLD) and serve the frontend over the resulting `https://myapp.test`, or
- If you use Laravel Herd or Valet, run `herd secure` / `valet secure` for the site,
  or
- Serve the frontend directly over `https://localhost:<port>` instead of a custom
  domain.

Testing from a phone or another device on your LAN needs the same fix — a bare LAN
IP over HTTP will never satisfy the secure-context check, no matter how the backend
is configured.

## 2. RP ID and allowed origins must match the serving origin exactly

`config/passkeys.php` ships with:

- `PASSKEY_RP_ID` — must be the origin's own host, or a registrable **parent**
  domain of it. Never a sibling host. If the frontend and API are on different
  subdomains (`app.example.com` and `api.example.com`), set this to the shared
  parent (`example.com`).
- `PASSKEY_RP_NAME` — display name only, cosmetic.
- `PASSKEY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` — a comma-separated list of **full origins**, including
  scheme and port (e.g. `https://app.example.com`), never a bare host.

**Failure mode if you get this wrong:** the browser refuses the ceremony with a
`SecurityError` **before any authenticator prompt appears at all**. There is no
server-side log to point at this — from the user's side it looks exactly like
nothing happened. If passkeys silently "do nothing" in a given environment, this
misconfiguration is the first thing to check, not a client-side bug.

**Action required:** set all three environment variables per environment (local,
staging, production) before testing passkeys there, and confirm `PASSKEY_RP_ID`
matches the actual host serving the frontend in that environment — not the one you
copied from a previous environment's `.env`.

## 3. Passkeys need a shared cache store, not `array` or per-node `file`

Both ceremonies store their single-use challenge in `Cache`, keyed by user id
(registration) or by a server-issued `ceremony_id` (login), with a 5-minute TTL,
read back once via `Cache::pull()`.

**Action required:** confirm `CACHE_STORE` in every environment that runs more than
one node (or more than one PHP-FPM worker pool behind a load balancer) points at a
shared store — Redis, Memcached, or your database cache driver — not `array` (which
never persists across requests) and not `file` (which is local to whichever node
wrote it). If it does not, the completion request for a ceremony can land on a node
that never saw the original challenge, and registration/login will intermittently
fail with `passkey_challenge_expired` for no reason visible in that request's own
logs.

## 4. `web-auth/webauthn-lib` dependency note

This package pins `web-auth/webauthn-lib: ^5.3`. The library is actively maintained
but has a single dominant maintainer (bus factor 1), and a `6.0` release with a
published migration guide exists upstream while `5.4`/`6.0` are not yet released as
of this writing. All contact with the library is confined to one generated file,
`src/Authentication/Domain/Services/PasskeyCeremonyService.php`, specifically so
that a future 5.x → 6.0 upgrade is a one-file change rather than a scan of every
generated Action.

**Action, when you eventually see a `web-auth/webauthn-lib` major-version bump
available:** read the migration guide at
<https://webauthn-doc.spomky-labs.com/migration/from-v5.x-to-v6.0> before
upgrading, and expect to touch only `PasskeyCeremonyService.php`. No action is
needed today solely because of this note — it is here so the eventual upgrade
isn't a surprise, not because `^5.3` is currently broken or unsupported.

## Passkey login and the 2FA feature

If you also generated the two-factor-authentication feature: a successful passkey
login returns a real `token_type: "Bearer"` directly, bypassing the generated 2FA
challenge pipeline (`google2fa.php`'s pipes only run inside the password login
action). This is deliberate — the passkey stands in for the second factor rather
than adding to it. If you want both factors to stack instead, add
`PasskeyLoginAction`'s equivalent of the 2FA pipe yourself; this package does not
generate that wiring.
